.Dd $Mdocdate: May 29 2026 $
.Dt HPNSFTP-SERVER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hpnsftp-server
.Nd HPN/OpenSSH SFTP server subsystem
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm hpnsftp-server
.Bk -words
.Op Fl ehR
.Op Fl d Ar start_directory
.Op Fl f Ar log_facility
.Op Fl l Ar log_level
.Op Fl P Ar denied_requests
.Op Fl p Ar allowed_requests
.Op Fl u Ar umask
.Ek
.Nm
.Fl Q Ar protocol_feature
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a program that speaks the server side of SFTP protocol
to stdout and expects client requests from stdin.
.Nm
is not intended to be called directly, but from
.Xr hpnsshd 8
using the
.Cm Subsystem
option.
.Pp
Command-line flags to
.Nm
should be specified in the
.Cm Subsystem
declaration.
See
.Xr hpnsshd_config 5
for more information.
.Pp
Valid options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d Ar start_directory
Specifies an alternate starting directory for users.
The pathname may contain the following tokens that are expanded at runtime:
%% is replaced by a literal '%',
%d is replaced by the home directory of the user being authenticated,
and %u is replaced by the username of that user.
The default is to use the user's home directory.
This option is useful in conjunction with the
.Xr sshd_config 5
.Cm ChrootDirectory
option.
.It Fl e
Causes
.Nm
to print logging information to stderr instead of syslog for debugging.
.It Fl f Ar log_facility
Specifies the facility code that is used when logging messages from
.Nm .
The possible values are: DAEMON, USER, AUTH, LOCAL0, LOCAL1, LOCAL2,
LOCAL3, LOCAL4, LOCAL5, LOCAL6, LOCAL7.
The default is AUTH.
.It Fl h
Displays
.Nm
usage information.
.It Fl l Ar log_level
Specifies which messages will be logged by
.Nm .
The possible values are:
QUIET, FATAL, ERROR, INFO, VERBOSE, DEBUG, DEBUG1, DEBUG2, and DEBUG3.
INFO and VERBOSE log transactions that
.Nm
performs on behalf of the client.
DEBUG and DEBUG1 are equivalent.
DEBUG2 and DEBUG3 each specify higher levels of debugging output.
The default is ERROR.
.It Fl P Ar denied_requests
Specifies a comma-separated list of SFTP protocol requests that are banned by
the server.
.Nm
will reply to any denied request with a failure.
The
.Fl Q
flag can be used to determine the supported request types.
If both denied and allowed lists are specified, then the denied list is
applied before the allowed list.
.It Fl p Ar allowed_requests
Specifies a comma-separated list of SFTP protocol requests that are permitted
by the server.
All request types that are not on the allowed list will be logged and replied
to with a failure message.
.Pp
Care must be taken when using this feature to ensure that requests made
implicitly by SFTP clients are permitted.
.Pp
The HPN-SSH server-side hashing extensions
.Cm hpn-check-file
and
.Cm sftp-hash-range
are additionally governed by the
.Cm read
policy, the layout extension
.Cm hpn-file-layout
by the
.Cm write
policy, and the filesystem-info extension
.Cm hpn-fs-info
by the
.Cm statvfs
policy, because they act on file contents or metadata directly rather than
through the standard read and write requests.
Denying
.Cm read ,
.Cm write ,
or
.Cm statvfs
therefore also denies the corresponding extension; each may also be named
explicitly.
.Pp
Denying these extensions disables the HPN-SSH features that depend on them.
Without
.Cm hpn-check-file
and
.Cm sftp-hash-range
the server will not hash files on the client's behalf, so post-transfer
integrity verification and hash-verified resume of interrupted transfers
become unavailable; affected transfers fall back to a size-based resume or a
full re-transfer, and the client cannot cryptographically confirm that the
bytes on the server match the source.
Without
.Cm hpn-file-layout
the client cannot set a storage layout on the destination, so files are
written with the filesystem's default layout; on a parallel filesystem such
as Lustre this can substantially reduce large-file throughput.
Without
.Cm hpn-fs-info
the client cannot query the destination filesystem's type, optimal block
size, and stripe geometry, so it falls back to generic defaults and forgoes
filesystem-aware tuning such as detecting Lustre to select a stripe layout.
Ordinary reads and writes are otherwise unaffected, so denying these
extensions trades those acceleration and integrity features for the tighter
policy.
.It Fl Q Ar protocol_feature
Queries protocol features supported by
.Nm .
At present the only feature that may be queried is
.Dq requests ,
which may be used to deny or allow specific requests (flags
.Fl P
and
.Fl p
respectively).
.It Fl R
Places this instance of
.Nm
into a read-only mode.
Attempts to open files for writing, as well as other operations that change
the state of the filesystem, will be denied.
.It Fl u Ar umask
Sets an explicit
.Xr umask 2
to be applied to newly-created files and directories, instead of the
user's default mask.
.El
.Pp
On some systems,
.Nm
must be able to access
.Pa /dev/log
for logging to work, and use of
.Nm
in a chroot configuration therefore requires that
.Xr syslogd 8
establish a logging socket inside the chroot directory.
.Sh HPN-SSH SFTP EXTENSIONS
In addition to the SFTP extensions inherited from upstream OpenSSH,
.Nm
advertises the following HPN-specific extensions in its
.Sy SSH_FXP_VERSION
reply.
A
.Xr hpnsftp 1
client uses the advertisements to decide whether each feature is
available; non-HPN clients ignore them.
.Pp
These extensions require a compatible peer (HPN-SSH 19.0 or later) on
both ends.
When the client side is an HPN-SSH 19.0 or later
.Xr hpnsftp 1
and the server does not advertise an extension, the client falls back to
the closest equivalent path that does not require it; when the server is
19.0 or later but the client is older or non-HPN, the extensions remain
advertised but unused.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy hpn-check-file@hpnssh.org
Compute an XXH3_64 hash over a path's first
.Ar length
bytes and return it to the client.
Used by the verified-resume code paths in
.Xr hpnsftp 1
.Pq Fl v
to decide whether an existing destination file matches the source.
The hash is always computed by reading the path's bytes directly from disk,
using direct I/O to bypass the page cache, so the returned hash reflects what
is actually stored on the media rather than what may still be buffered in
memory.
.It Sy sftp-hash-range@hpnssh.org
Compute XXH3_64 hashes over a list of byte ranges of a path in a single
request/response round trip.
Used by chunked verified-resume to identify exactly which 64 MiB chunks of
a same-size destination differ from the source, so only those chunks are
re-transferred instead of the whole file.
This is the
.Dq sparse-zero hole
recovery path for crashed parallel-streams uploads.
.It Sy hpn-fs-info@hpnssh.org
Return the filesystem type and, for striped parallel filesystems
(Lustre, GPFS), the stripe geometry of a given path.
Used by
.Xr hpnsftp 1
to align byte-range parallel transfers
.Pq Fl j , Fl M
to stripe boundaries on the destination.
.It Sy hpn-bundle@hpnssh.org
Capability advertisement for tar-stream bundling of small files on
upload, carried out by the
.Sy hpn-bundle-open@hpnssh.org
request below.
.It Sy hpn-bundle-open@hpnssh.org
Open a destination bundle for upload.
The client names the target directory, which the server creates if
necessary, and then streams a whole set of small files into it as a
single tar stream instead of transferring each file separately.
This is the upload counterpart to
.Sy hpn-bundle-fetch@hpnssh.org .
Reduces per-file round trips on high-RTT uploads of many small files.
.It Sy hpn-bundle-fetch@hpnssh.org
Server-side bundling of small files for download: the server tar-streams
a set of files into a single bundle that the client then reads back.
Reduces per-file open/close round trips on high-RTT downloads of many
small files.
.It Sy hpn-file-layout@hpnssh.org
.Sy EXPERIMENTAL :
apply a filesystem-specific layout to a destination directory before
files are created in it.
Today the only supported backend is Lustre; the server uses the
.Sy LL_IOC_LOV_SETSTRIPE
ioctl directly on the open directory file descriptor, no
.Sy liblustreapi
runtime dependency.
Used by
.Xr hpnsftp 1
to set the stripe count on the destination directory of a parallel
.Pq Fl j
upload so subsequent file creations
.Pq including bundled files extracted by libarchive
inherit the wider layout automatically.
See
.Cm HPNLustreStripeCount
in
.Xr hpnssh_config 5
for the client-side configuration.
The generic extension name is deliberate: it leaves room to add GPFS
or BeeGFS layout control in a future revision using the same wire
shape.
On non-Lustre filesystems the server reports
.Dq not on a layout-capable filesystem
without modifying anything;
.Xr hpnsftp 1
treats that response as a silent no-op.
.It Sy hpn-max-workers@hpnssh.org
Advertise the operator's per-user cap on the number of concurrent
parallel-transfer workers.
The value is a decimal count;
.Dq 0
means no cap, which is also what a standalone server with no configured
limit sends.
The advertisement is advisory: an
.Xr hpnsftp 1
client uses it to self-limit a parallel
.Pq Fl j
transfer so it does not open more workers than the operator permits.
A stock OpenSSH server never sends it, which the client reads as
.Dq no policy
and applies its own conservative default.
See
.Cm HPNMaxConcurrentWorkers
in
.Xr hpnsshd_config 5
for the server-side configuration.
.El
.Pp
For long-running hash extensions
.Pq Sy hpn-check-file , sftp-hash-range
the server emits a small
.Dq still working
heartbeat reply on a fixed cadence while the hash is in progress.
The client refreshes its parallel-streams watchdog grace on each heartbeat,
which prevents the orchestrator from killing a worker that is legitimately
waiting on a slow or contended-storage hash.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr hpnsftp 1 ,
.Xr hpnssh 1 ,
.Xr hpnsshd_config 5 ,
.Xr hpnsshd 8
